␀␀␀␀␀{
  "schema": "eloria.encyclopedia/2",
  "title": "The Rabbit Lands Encyclopedia",
  "note": "IN DEVELOPMENT! Every page here is either original Rabbit Lands reference text or is built from the client's own catalogues, so a recipe, spell or book that changes in the data changes here too. Report corrections through the Rabbit Lands project issue tracker.",
  "skillNotes": {
    "attack": "How hard you hit and how often you connect. The server rolls it; the client only shows what it said happened.",
    "defense": "How often a blow misses you, and how much of one your armour is allowed to soak.",
    "harvesting": "How quickly you take from a resource node, and which nodes the server will let you start on at all.",
    "alchemy": "Essences, bars, and the ingredients the rest of the trades are built out of.",
    "magic": "The ceiling on which spells you may cast, and on the power you may ask for.",
    "potion": "Everything that ends up in a vial, from the plain healing draught upwards.",
    "summoning": "Calling a creature to fight beside you, and the stones and flesh it costs.",
    "manufacturing": "Metal work: armour, weapons, and the plates and rings they are made from.",
    "crafting": "Rings, medallions and the small worked goods that need a mould.",
    "engineering": "Devices and traps, and the tools that build them.",
    "tailoring": "Thread, cloth, dye, and every garment made from them.",
    "ranging": "Shooting at distance. The server refuses a shot at a target standing too close.",
    "overall": "Not a trade of its own. It is the sum the server keeps across all the others."
  },
  "categories": [
    {
      "id": "world",
      "title": "The World",
      "summary": "Nymara, its hours and its weather",
      "intro": "Eloria is played on Nymara. The server owns the world and the clock; this client draws what it is told and asks for the rest.",
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "authority",
          "title": "What the server decides",
          "summary": "Why nothing here is a guess",
          "body": "Every fact in this client came from the server: where you stand, what you carry, what you hit and what hit you. The client picks the artwork and the camera, and nothing else.\n\nThat is worth knowing when a click seems to do nothing. Asking to walk somewhere, to attack someone, or to take from a node is a request. The server answers it, and the answer may be no. What you see on screen is the answer, not the request."
        },
        {
          "id": "time",
          "title": "Day and night",
          "summary": "Six real hours to a Nymaran day",
          "body": "A full day is 360 game minutes and one game minute is one real minute, so the world turns through dawn, noon, dusk and night over six hours.\n\nThe light you see follows the same curve the server uses to decide how far creatures can be seen, so a dark night is dark for both of you.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Length of a day",
              "360 game minutes"
            ],
            [
              "In real time",
              "6 hours"
            ],
            [
              "Where to see it",
              "The clock face on the lower HUD"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "markers",
          "title": "Marks and waypoints",
          "summary": "The server's marks and your own",
          "body": "A waypoint is the server's: it appears on your map and reports the distance as you move.\n\nA mark is your own. It never leaves this client, and it is drawn in a different colour so you can tell your own note from something the world told you."
        },
        {
          "id": "weather",
          "title": "Weather and light",
          "summary": "Rain, cloud, and the sky over each map",
          "body": "Weather belongs to the map you are standing on. The server states it, the client renders it, and it changes the light rather than only the picture.\n\nAn interior does not take the sky's weather. Walking indoors changes what lights you and what you can hear, which is why a doorway can feel like a different time of day."
        },
        {
          "id": "world-weather-todo",
          "title": "Weather and night do nothing yet",
          "summary": "TODO - not written yet",
          "sections": [
            {
              "title": "Not written yet",
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "A page describing what weather and nightfall do to play would be describing nothing. Weather is a wet chance, a storm chance and an intensity, sent so the client can draw rain; the day and night cycle has no server-side effect at all. The axis has to exist before a page can explain it."
            },
            {
              "title": "What it needs",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Have",
                  "Rain drawn from a per-map climate"
                ],
                [
                  "Need",
                  "Weather and night that change play"
                ],
                [
                  "Where",
                  "dev-server/eloria/weather.py"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "body": "TODO. A page describing what weather and nightfall do to play would be describing nothing. Weather is a wet chance, a storm chance and an intensity, sent so the client can draw rain; the day and night cycle has no server-side effect at all. The axis has to exist before a page can explain it.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Have",
              "Rain drawn from a per-map climate"
            ],
            [
              "Need",
              "Weather and night that change play"
            ],
            [
              "Where",
              "dev-server/eloria/weather.py"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "skills",
      "title": "Skills",
      "summary": "The thirteen the server keeps for you",
      "intro": "The server keeps a level and an experience total for each of these. The Statistics window shows all of them at once; the lower HUD shows the one you asked it to follow.",
      "generate": "skills",
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "skills-todo",
          "title": "Skill pages are one line deep",
          "summary": "TODO - not written yet",
          "sections": [
            {
              "title": "Not written yet",
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "Every skill has a page, and each says what the server reports and little else. What a skill affects and how it is trained are not recorded anywhere - they are design knowledge that has never been written down, so there is nothing to generate them from."
            },
            {
              "title": "What it needs",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Have",
                  "A level, an experience total, and a one-line note"
                ],
                [
                  "Need",
                  "What each skill affects, and how it is trained"
                ],
                [
                  "Where",
                  "skillNotes in this document"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "body": "TODO. Every skill has a page, and each says what the server reports and little else. What a skill affects and how it is trained are not recorded anywhere - they are design knowledge that has never been written down, so there is nothing to generate them from.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Have",
              "A level, an experience total, and a one-line note"
            ],
            [
              "Need",
              "What each skill affects, and how it is trained"
            ],
            [
              "Where",
              "skillNotes in this document"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "combat",
      "title": "Combat",
      "summary": "Attacking, ranging, and being hit",
      "intro": "Combat is entirely the server's. This client asks it to start a fight and then reports what came back.",
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "attacking",
          "title": "Attacking",
          "summary": "Three ways to start a fight",
          "body": "There are three ways to ask for a fight, and all three send the same request.\n\nHold Alt and click a living creature or player. The move icon on the lower HUD turns into the attack icon while Alt is held, so you can see what a click will do before you make it; let Alt go and it turns back.\n\nOr click the attack icon on the HUD. That latches: the icon stays lit and every click attacks until you choose the move icon again, and holding or releasing Alt does not undo it.\n\nOr select a target and press the attack key.\n\nWhatever you choose, the server walks you into reach, decides whether the fight may start, and refuses the ones that may not.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Hold Alt and click",
              "attacks without changing mode"
            ],
            [
              "Attack icon",
              "stays on until you pick the move icon"
            ],
            [
              "Attack key",
              "attacks the selected target"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ranging",
          "title": "Ranging",
          "summary": "Bows, arrows and distance",
          "body": "A bow and matching ammunition let you attack from a distance. The server refuses a shot at a target standing too close - move away and try again.\n\nEach shot is announced twice: once as an aim, once as the arrow leaving. The damage is resolved by the server and arrives separately."
        },
        {
          "id": "armour",
          "title": "Armour and damage",
          "summary": "What a suit is allowed to soak",
          "body": "Armour is worn in pieces - helm, torso, legs, boots and a shield - and each piece carries its own absorption and its own penalties. A heavy suit soaks more, and costs you accuracy and movement to wear.\n\nWhat a piece absorbs is what the server allows it to take off a blow, not what it will take off every blow. The roll is still the server's."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "perks",
      "title": "Perks",
      "entries": [
        {
          "id": "perks-overview",
          "title": "How perks work",
          "summary": "Pick points, tiers, exclusions and gear",
          "body": "A perk is bought with pick points, and most of them are bought more than once. There are 32 perks on the shelf and 32 of them come in tiers.\n\nPick points come from overall levels: two for each, spent on attributes, nexus or perks, and never spent twice. Every tier of a perk costs 3 pick points, so owning one outright is 9; what climbs from tier to tier is the gold.\n\nSome perks cannot be held together - Lucky Hitter and Lucky Dodger answer the same question from opposite sides, and so do Mirror and Life Stealing. The page for each perk says what it clashes with.\n\nWorn gear can carry a perk. A cape does not simply grant one: it adds a tier, which means it is worth the same to a player who has bought all three as to one who has bought none - and it is the only way to reach the tiers above the third.\n\nA removal stone takes back one tier and returns what that tier cost. At its highest tier a perk that says so can be switched off with the #perk command, which hands back the effect and not the pick points.\n\n#list_perks names everything you hold, the tier you are at, and what that tier does.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Perks",
              "32"
            ],
            [
              "Tiered",
              "32"
            ],
            [
              "Tiers each",
              "3"
            ],
            [
              "A tier costs",
              "3 pick points"
            ],
            [
              "Highest with gear",
              "5"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "perk-armor-piercing",
          "title": "Armor Piercing",
          "summary": "Fighting - Three fifths of a target's armour is ignored.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "title": "How it works",
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "Bought in 3 tiers. Each tier replaces the one below it rather than adding to it, so the effect shown for a tier is the whole of what the perk does at that tier."
            },
            {
              "title": "Tiers",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Tier 1",
                  "3 pick points, 100 gold. A fifth of a target's armour is ignored, in melee and at range."
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 2",
                  "3 pick points, 4,000 gold. Two fifths of a target's armour is ignored."
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 3",
                  "3 pick points, 20,000 gold. Three fifths of a target's armour is ignored."
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "title": "Worn gear",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Carried by",
                  "Body Piercing Cloak, Piercer's Cape"
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 4",
                  "Three quarters of a target's armour is ignored."
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 5",
                  "Nine tenths of a target's armour is ignored."
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "title": "Also",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Taken back by",
                  "Armor Piercing Removal"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "related": [],
          "body": "Bought in 3 tiers, each one costing 3 pick points and more gold than the last.\n\nTier 1 - 3 pick points, 100 gold. A fifth of a target's armour is ignored, in melee and at range.\nTier 2 - 3 pick points, 4,000 gold. Two fifths of a target's armour is ignored.\nTier 3 - 3 pick points, 20,000 gold. Three fifths of a target's armour is ignored.\n\nWorn gear adds tiers on top of what pick points buy, so this perk goes further than tier 3 for anybody who owns the right piece:\nTier 4 - Three quarters of a target's armour is ignored.\nTier 5 - Nine tenths of a target's armour is ignored.\n\nCarried by: Body Piercing Cloak, Piercer's Cape.",
          "facts": [
            [
              "Tiers",
              "3"
            ],
            [
              "All tiers",
              "9 pick points"
            ],
            [
              "Gold",
              "24,100 gold"
            ],
            [
              "Gear",
              "Body Piercing Cloak, Piercer's Cape"
            ],
            [
              "Taken back by",
              "Armor Piercing Removal"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "perk-berserker",
          "title": "Berserker",
          "summary": "Fighting - Twenty more damage on every blow, and twenty defense lost.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "title": "How it works",
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "Bought in 3 tiers. Each tier replaces the one below it rather than adding to it, so the effect shown for a tier is the whole of what the perk does at that tier."
            },
            {
              "title": "Tiers",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Tier 1",
                  "3 pick points, 400 gold. Five more damage on every blow you land, and five defense lost."
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 2",
                  "3 pick points, 3,000 gold. Ten more damage on every blow, and ten defense lost."
                ],
                [
                  "Tier 3",
                  "3 pick points, 15,000 gold. Twenty more damage on every blow, and twenty defense lost."
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "title": "Also",
              "kind": "facts",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Taken back by",
                  "Berserker Removal"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "related": [],
          "body": "Bought in 3 tiers, each one costing 3 pick points and more gold than the last.\n\nTier 1 - 3 pick points, 400 gold. Five more damage on every blow you land, and five defense lost.\nTier 2 - 3 pick points, 3,000 gold. Ten more damage on every blow, and ten defense lost.\nTier 3 - 3 pick points, 15,000 gold. Twenty more damage on every blow, and twenty defense lost.",
          "facts": [
